WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.200
I'm Georgina from BBC Learning English.

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.780
Have you ever thought about the difference between

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:07.920
'lend' and 'borrow'?

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:11.400
They are both verbs but they're used in different ways.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:14.400
'Lend' means 'to give something to someone'

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.160
generally for a short time.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:18.072
For example:

00:00:18.072 --> 00:00:20.240
The bank lent me some money.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:23.980
If you want to say who you lent something to,

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:26.940
you can use the preposition 'to'.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:30.740
I lent my mobile phone to James.

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:34.600
Oh no! I forgot my pen. Can you lend me a pen?

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:37.700
'Borrow' means 'to ask someone for something'.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:41.560
Oh no! I can't read this. Can I borrow your glasses?

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:44.660
If you want to say who you borrowed something from,

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:47.340
you can use the preposition 'from'.

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:48.360
For example:

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:51.060
I borrowed a book from the library.

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:52.420
Oh, I'm late!

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:55.880
I need to borrow a laptop from Sam for my meeting

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:56.760
– got to go!

